  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates downstream humor-reasoning performance across models of different sizes, a different measurement from the original pretraining-loss scaling experiments.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports downstream benchmark results by model and discusses their relationship to model scale. Its cited descriptions of Kaplan et al. are not results from this study. Context-length comparisons, English-versus-Hindi comparisons, TTR, and human evaluation do not test the scale-to-downstream-performance relationship in V6.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares off-the-shelf LLMs from 7B to 70B, plus a frontier model, on a new Hindi humor-explanation benchmark. It explicitly reads the results against Kaplan-style scaling, which bears on V6 in a new downstream setting. The evidence is weak: models differ in family, data and training, no loss or compute is reported, and nothing is fitted.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. The authors say performance rises with model size, but the gains across 7B-to-frontier are meagre, and this shows a limit to scaling for pragmatic humor tasks. Language specialization (Sarvam-M) matters, metrics disagree, and human evaluation of Sarvam-M gives only 26.25% accuracy.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates Hindi humor explanations on a downstream benchmark across existing models, rather than measuring held-out pretraining loss in controlled scaling runs.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Performance depends on metric and model: Llama-3.1-70B leads Qwen-3-7B on BLEU but trails it on BERTScore; the authors also report advantages for Indic-specialized Sarvam-M-24B over larger generalist models on nearly all metrics.",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Proceedings of the 4th Workshop on Cross-Cultural Considerations in NLP (C3NLP 2026)",
    "problems": "The model comparison does not isolate parameter count from architecture, training data, language specialization, or other model differences. The reported table gives no uncertainty estimates, and the authors note that standard overlap metrics do not reliably capture pragmatic humor understanding.",
    "rows_left_out": "No rows were omitted from the selected Table 2 downstream benchmark metrics. TTR was excluded because it is a linguistic-diversity measure, not a downstream performance measure. English-versus-Hindi comparisons, context-length results, and the single-model human evaluation do not test V6's scale-to-performance relationship.",
    "untested": [
